<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Debrecen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Debrecen is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, in the east of the country. A Calvinist centre since the 16th century, it served as the seat of the revolutionary government in 1849, when the Hungarian parliament proclaimed independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in its Great Reformed Church. In December 1944 a provisional national government was formed there under Soviet protection.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/debrecen.jpg" alt="Debrecen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Björn Láczay · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Universit%C3%A4t_Debrecen_Nr._3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Debrecen is first documented in 1235 as Debrezun. It became more important when small villages nearby were deserted during the Mongol invasion, and it grew rapidly after the middle of the 13th century. In 1361 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> granted its citizens the right to choose the town's judge and council. During the Ottoman period it had no castle or city walls and survived through the diplomatic skill of its leaders. It came under Ottoman control as a sanjak between 1558 and 1693, and in 1693 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-holy-roman-emperor">Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, raised it to free royal town status.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> took root in the 16th century, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and then Calvin's teachings. From 1551 the Calvinist government forbade Catholics from moving to the town, and a synod in 1567 adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-helvetic-confession">Second Helvetic Confession</a>. The town earned the names the Calvinist Rome and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and its Reformed College, founded in 1538, was a predecessor of today's University of Debrecen.</p><p>In 1849 the Hungarian revolutionary government fled to Debrecen from Pest-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>. In April, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a> proclaimed the dethronement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> at the Great Church, and Russian forces allied to the Habsburgs defeated the Hungarian army near the town in the last battle of the war of independence.</p><p>German forces entered the city on 20 March 1944. A ghetto was ordered on 28 April, and in June the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Debrecen were deported to a nearby brickyard; most were later deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>, arriving on 3 July. Soviet troops captured the city on 20 October 1944, and 70 percent of its buildings were damaged in the fighting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Debrecen is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s second-largest city, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, on the Great Hungarian Plain 220 km east of the capital, and the seat of Hajdú-Bihar County. The University of Debrecen, formed in 2000 from three earlier universities, is the largest in Hungary. The Reformed Great Church can accommodate about 5,000 people, and the park of the Nagyerdő lies in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Debrecen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Debrecen</b> government began forming a new army to fight on the Soviet side, but the volunteers made up only small units, the largest being the Volunteer Regiment of Buda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A rival provisional government was formed at <b>Debrecen</b> under Soviet protection in December 1944.</blockquote><a href="/state/government-of-national-unity">Government of National Unity</a></li><li><blockquote>After the spring campaign drove back Austrian forces, the Hungarian parliament, meeting in <b>Debrecen</b>, adopted the Declaration of Independence on 14 April 1849, deposing the Habsburg dynasty and electing Lajos Kossuth governor-president.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-state-1849">Hungarian State (1849)</a></li></ul></section>
